  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Three masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> are arranged in a system as shown in figure. All the surfaces are frictionless and strings are inextensible and weightless. The pulleys are also weightless and frictionless. A force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math> is applied on the system so that the mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> moves upward with an acceleration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>ms</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math>&nbsp;is</p><p>(Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>ms</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>)</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0073_4486cbb7636e.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 137px;\" /></p>"
